# VOCABULARY — the words that belong to the brewery tenant and to no
# other tier (backlog be39298f).
#
# why: BOSS core is a generic state-machine toolkit; the brewery is one
# tenant instantiated on it (CLAUDE.md §10). Until 2026-09-16 the tier
# rule was enforced only as a Cargo dependency audit, and a grep for
# these words outside the brewery found them in every tier above it:
# kegs in core registries, excise in module ledgers, the brewery
# simulator in an orchestrator crate. This file is the TENANT declaring
# its own words, so the sensor that counts them
# (infra/lint/no-tenant-vocabulary-above-its-tier.sh) reads the list
# from the tenant rather than carrying an opinion about what a brewery
# is. A word here is one that names nothing but this tenant: a hit in
# crates/core is a leak by definition, never a coincidence.
#
# A line beginning with `!` declares the opposite: a PHRASE that is NOT
# this tenant's, though a term matches inside it. `brew*` is right in
# general and wrong for `brew install`, which names Homebrew — a builder
# writing macOS install steps was refused by it on 2026-09-20 and had to
# reword the step (backlog e9423392). The block below already made this
# call in prose for whole words; a `!` line makes it for a phrase, where
# the sensor can read it.
#
# Format: one term per line, matched case-insensitively. A trailing `*`
# matches any word beginning so (brew* = brew, brewery, brewhouse,
# brewing); without it the term is a whole word (wort, not worth).
# Terms are lowercase letters, digits, spaces and hyphens only.
#
# Deliberately NOT listed, because each names something outside the
# brewery too: tap (a network/route sense), batch, hop/hops (a routing
# hop), grain (coarse-grained), barrel (a generic container), ale (an
# alert's prefix), recipe, distributor, seasonal, diagnosis.
algedonic ales
brew*
beer
beers
keg*
wort
worts
excise*
taproom*
ferment*
malt*
mash
mashes
lager*
stout
stouts
cellar*
yeast*
bbl
ttb
!brew install
